---
import { calculator } from '../config/calculator.config';
import { site } from '../config/site.config';
import AdSlot from './AdSlot.astro';
---
<div class="shell content-grid"><div><section id="how-it-works" class="content-section"><span class="eyebrow">START WITH THE CIRCUIT</span><h2>Choose the model.<br />Understand the output.</h2><div class="steps">{[
  ['Select a rectifier model', 'Choose a capacitor-filtered bridge estimate, or an ideal unfiltered full-wave or half-wave model.'],
  ['Enter the AC input', 'Use sinusoidal RMS voltage at the rectifier input and its frequency. Filtered mode also needs diode drop, load current, and capacitance.'],
  ['Read the whole result', 'Compare average DC, peak, valley, ripple, and ripple frequency. The average alone does not describe a smooth or regulated supply.'],
].map(([title, text], i) => <div class="step"><span>{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span><div><h3>{title}</h3><p>{text}</p></div></div>)}</div></section>
<section id="formula" class="content-section"><span class="eyebrow">BEHIND THE ESTIMATE</span><h2>The formula depends on the filter.</h2><p>There is no universal multiplier from AC to DC. The selected circuit and output quantity determine the calculation.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Single-phase sinusoidal rectifier formulas</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Quantity</th><th scope="col">Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody>{calculator.formulas.map(([label, formula]) => <tr><th scope="row">{label}</th><td>{formula}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>Vrms and Vf are volts, I is amperes, f is input hertz, and C is farads. Entered µF is multiplied by 10⁻⁶. Bridge ripple repeats at 2f; half-wave ripple repeats at f.</p><h3>A worked example</h3><div class="example"><strong>12 V RMS → approximately 15.071 V DC</strong><p>With a bridge, 0.7 V per diode, 50 Hz AC, 0.1 A load, and 1,000 µF: available peak = 12√2 − 1.4 ≈ 15.571 V. Ripple = 0.1 ÷ (100 × 0.001) = 1 V p-p. Estimated average = 15.571 − 0.5 ≈ 15.071 V; valley ≈ 14.571 V.</p></div><h3>Derivation and references</h3><p>Ideal averages come from integrating a rectified sine over a complete cycle. Filtered ripple uses charge balance, ΔV ≈ IΔt/C, with Δt ≈ 1/(2f). The average estimate is the midpoint of the approximately linear discharge. This is not a circuit simulation.</p><p class="references">{calculator.references.map((ref, i) => <>{i > 0 ? ' · ' : ''}<a href={ref.url}>{ref.title}</a></>)}</p><h3>Assumptions and limits</h3><ul class="assumptions">{calculator.assumptions.map(item => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul><p>Inputs: 0–1,000 V RMS, 1–1,000 Hz; filtered mode: 0–2 V per diode, 0–100 A, 1–1,000,000 µF. These are software bounds, not equipment ratings.</p></section>
<section id="faq" class="content-section"><span class="eyebrow">GOOD QUESTIONS</span><h2>Know what you are calculating.</h2>{[
  ['Why 0.9 × AC sometimes, but near 1.414 × AC at other times?', 'About 0.9 × RMS is the ideal unfiltered full-wave average. A lightly loaded capacitor charges near the sine peak, about 1.414 × RMS before diode loss. These describe different circuits and quantities.'],
  ['Can I use this for a regulated AC adapter?', 'No. A regulated or switch-mode adapter has additional control and conversion stages. Its output cannot be inferred from this rectifier model.'],
  ['What does the diode drop setting mean?', 'The assumed forward drop of one conducting diode at the operating condition. A bridge conducts through two diodes. The 0.7 V default is illustrative; use relevant device data.'],
  ['Why is high ripple rejected?', 'The filtered model assumes small, approximately linear discharge between charging peaks. Ripple above 20% of the available peak is outside the tool’s chosen approximation scope.'],
  ['Can voltage tell me available DC current?', 'No. Voltage alone does not provide transformer capacity, thermal limits, charging-current stress, or available output power.'],
].map(([question, answer]) => <details><summary>{question}<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>{answer}</p></details>)}</section></div>
<aside class="sidebar"><AdSlot placement="sidebar" /><div class="aside-card"><span class="eyebrow">MODEL MATTERS</span><h3>One input.<br />Different outputs.</h3><p class="quick-equation">12 V RMS<span>≈ 10.804 V average</span><span>ideal full-wave, no filter</span></p><p>A capacitor-filtered bridge behaves differently. Always state the circuit and whether you mean peak, average, or RMS.</p><a href="/blog/ac-to-dc-formulas/">Compare the models ↗</a></div><div class="aside-card plain"><span class="eyebrow">RELATED TOOLS</span>{site.relatedTools.map(tool => <a class="related-tool" href={tool.href}><strong>{tool.title} ↗</strong><span>{tool.description}</span></a>)}</div></aside></div>
